WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.038
Machining means making a part by cutting material away, in small curls called chips.

2
00:00:07.587 --> 00:00:10.339
You start with a piece of metal that is too big,

3
00:00:10.539 --> 00:00:13.674
and cut until the shape on the drawing is left.

4
00:00:14.223 --> 00:00:19.706
It is precise and flexible, but every gram that comes off costs machine time.

5
00:00:20.606 --> 00:00:23.137
Follow this output shaft of a gearbox.

6
00:00:23.687 --> 00:00:27.950
It starts as a steel bar, 55 millimetres thick.

7
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.915
The routing is its travel plan: which machines, in which order.

8
00:00:33.815 --> 00:00:35.162
Step one: sawing.

9
00:00:35.712 --> 00:00:40.161
The bar is clamped, and the spinning blade comes down through it.

10
00:00:40.711 --> 00:00:42.987
In 1 minute it has the right length.

11
00:00:44.187 --> 00:00:48.880
Step two: the CNC lathe, a lathe controlled by a computer.

12
00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.043
The chuck grips the shaft, and spins it fast.

13
00:00:53.943 --> 00:00:58.996
That is turning: the part spins, and a fixed cutting tool moves along it.

14
00:00:59.896 --> 00:01:04.670
That is how you make everything round: shafts, bushes, flanges.

15
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.958
Here the tool turns the shaft down to 50 millimetres,

16
00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:11.620
with shoulders where the diameter steps.

17
00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.911
That takes 6 minutes.

18
00:01:14.811 --> 00:01:17.981
Note the bearing seat of 35 millimetres.

19
00:01:18.531 --> 00:01:23.036
There the turner deliberately leaves 0.2 millimetres extra.

20
00:01:23.585 --> 00:01:26.618
That only comes off later, in grinding.

21
00:01:27.818 --> 00:01:30.303
No machine makes a size exactly.

22
00:01:30.853 --> 00:01:35.079
So the drawing gives a tolerance: how far a size may deviate.

23
00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:41.218
Turning holds about 0.02 millimetres, plus or minus.

24
00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:49.187
So this 50 millimetre shoulder may come out between 49.98 and 50.02 millimetres.

25
00:01:50.387 --> 00:01:54.824
Step three: the machining centre, for everything that is not round.

26
00:01:55.375 --> 00:01:57.418
There it is the other way round.

27
00:01:57.968 --> 00:02:00.371
In milling the tool spins: the cutter.

28
00:02:01.271 --> 00:02:05.952
The part is clamped on the table, and the table moves it under the cutter.

29
00:02:06.502 --> 00:02:10.626
That is how you make flat faces, slots and recesses.

30
00:02:11.526 --> 00:02:17.555
Here it mills a keyway: a slot 10 millimetres wide in the shaft, in 3 minutes.

31
00:02:18.455 --> 00:02:22.733
Later a key, a small steel block, sits in it.

32
00:02:23.283 --> 00:02:27.314
That way a gear turns with the shaft, and does not slip.

33
00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:32.580
Drilling is mostly done on the same machine, with a spinning drill.

34
00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:38.160
But a drilled hole always comes out a touch too big, by a few hundredths.

35
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:43.393
If the hole must be exact, you first drill slightly small.

36
00:02:43.943 --> 00:02:51.214
Then a reamer takes off the last 0.1 to 0.3 millimetres, and the hole is to size.

37
00:02:52.414 --> 00:02:56.942
Step four: the shaft goes to an outside heat treater for 3 days.

38
00:02:57.492 --> 00:03:01.257
It heats the steel, and then cools it very fast.

39
00:03:02.157 --> 00:03:06.803
Hard steel wears much less, and that is what you want under a bearing.

40
00:03:07.354 --> 00:03:10.198
But hardening warps the shaft very slightly.

41
00:03:10.748 --> 00:03:13.093
So the final size comes after.

42
00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:16.766
Step five: the cylindrical grinder.

43
00:03:17.316 --> 00:03:22.264
The shaft turns between two centres, against a fast-spinning wheel.

44
00:03:23.164 --> 00:03:29.041
In grinding hard grains take off tiny amounts, like very fine sandpaper.

45
00:03:29.591 --> 00:03:35.434
That works on hardened steel too, where an ordinary cutting tool soon wears out.

46
00:03:36.334 --> 00:03:39.887
That is how the last 0.2 millimetres comes off,

47
00:03:40.087 --> 00:03:45.671
to 0.005 millimetres or better: four times tighter than turning.

48
00:03:46.221 --> 00:03:47.452
In 4 minutes.

49
00:03:48.352 --> 00:03:52.508
Because the bearing seat allows only 16 micrometres.

50
00:03:53.058 --> 00:03:55.960
Grinding is slow and needs its own machine,

51
00:03:56.161 --> 00:03:59.168
so you grind only where the drawing demands it.

52
00:04:00.368 --> 00:04:02.539
Look again at the finished shaft.

53
00:04:03.089 --> 00:04:05.225
Each shape has its own machine.

54
00:04:05.775 --> 00:04:08.016
The round parts come from the lathe,

55
00:04:08.916 --> 00:04:12.912
the keyway from the cutter, and the hole from drill and reamer,

56
00:04:13.812 --> 00:04:16.122
and the bearing seat from the grinder.

57
00:04:17.022 --> 00:04:19.878
All the cutting together takes 14 minutes.

58
00:04:20.428 --> 00:04:24.759
But from start to finish it takes about 6 days: mostly hardening,

59
00:04:24.959 --> 00:04:26.480
transport and waiting.

60
00:04:27.029 --> 00:04:31.084
Read the routing, and you see where the time really goes.
